## Safe withdrawal (detox)

If you are dependent on alcohol, you may need **medically supported withdrawal**. This usually means taking a reducing course of medicine, such as **chlordiazepoxide**, for about a week to prevent dangerous withdrawal symptoms. It can often be done **at home**, with regular check-ins, but some people need to do it in hospital or a specialist unit.

You will usually be given **thiamine** (vitamin B1) to protect your brain.

## Medicines to help you stay off alcohol

- **Acamprosate** reduces cravings.
- **Naltrexone** reduces the pleasure from drinking and cravings.
- **Disulfiram** makes you feel very unwell if you drink.

Your doctor will talk through which might suit you.

## Talking therapies

- **CBT** helps you understand your drinking and build new ways of coping
- **motivational interviewing** helps you find your own reasons for change
- **family or couples therapy** involves the people close to you

## Support groups

- **Alcoholics Anonymous (AA)**
- **SMART Recovery**, which uses practical tools from CBT
- **Al-Anon**, for family and friends of people who drink

## Other health problems

Your GP may check your **liver** and other health, and help with any **mental health** problems, which often improve once drinking is under control.

Next, read about [recovery from alcohol dependence](/conditions/mental-health/alcohol-dependence/living-well/).
